7. Develop a "designer's eye"
This is probably the most important part.

When you see a UI, train yourself to notice:

Spacing

Is the padding consistent?

Is there enough whitespace?

Typography

Are there too many fonts?

Is the hierarchy obvious?

Is the line height comfortable?

Color

Is there a clear primary color?

Is the contrast accessible?

Are colors being used intentionally?

Layout

Does everything align?

Is the content hierarchy obvious?

Does it work on mobile?

Interaction

What happens when I hover?

What happens when I click?

What happens when something fails?

Is the loading state clear?

Great frontend designers think about all of these, not just how the page looks.

8. Learn from excellent work
Study products and design systems from companies such as:

Apple

Stripe

Linear

Vercel

Airbnb

Shopify

GitHub

Don't simply copy their appearance. Study why their interfaces work.

9. Your daily practice
If you can spend 2–3 hours a day, I'd structure it like this:

30 min: Study a design

30 min: Learn frontend concepts

60–90 min: Build something

15 min: Review your work and identify what looks wrong

Do this consistently for a year and you'll be dramatically better than someone who only watches tutorials.

The skill stack I'd aim for
                 FRONTEND DESIGNER
                        │
        ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
        │               │               │
      DESIGN          CODE             UX
        │               │               │
   Typography       HTML/CSS        Usability
   Color            JavaScript      Accessibility
   Layout           React           Interaction
   Figma            TypeScript      Responsive
   Design systems   Next.js         User flows

Most important advice
Don't try to become "the best frontend designer" by learning more technologies.

Become the person who can take:

an idea → a beautiful design → clean responsive code → a polished real product

That's a much more valuable skill.
